When you type a URL into your web browser (acting as the client), it sends an HTTP or HTTPS request over the internet to a remote server. The server processes this request—retrieving fixed files for a static website or querying a database to generate real-time content for a dynamic website—and sends back a response containing HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Finally, your browser receives, parses, and renders these files to display the fully loaded webpage on your screen.

Key Definitions & Concepts for Your Assignment
HTTP vs. HTTPS:

HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol): The standard protocol used to transmit data between a web browser and a website over the internet (unencrypted).

HTTPS (HTTP Secure): The secure, encrypted version of HTTP that protects data privacy and integrity using SSL/TLS certificates.

Static vs. Dynamic Websites:

Static Website: Consists of fixed files (HTML/CSS) stored on the server that look the same for every visitor.

Dynamic Website: Generates content in real-time using server-side code and a database, allowing personalized user experiences (e.g., login dashboards, e-commerce stores).

The Roles:

Client: The device/browser initiating the request (you).

Server: The remote computer hosting the website files and responding to requests.

Database: A structured system that stores, organizes, and retrieves application data for dynamic sites.

How to Get Your Network Tab Screenshot
Open Google Chrome and go to any website.

Press F12 (or right-click anywhere and select Inspect).

Click on the Network tab at the top of the DevTools panel.

Refresh the page (F5) to capture all incoming requests (HTML, CSS, images, scripts).

